WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.200
The first film traced how Ras, Raf, MEK, and ERK carry a membrane signal to genes.

2
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.040
How can that same relay tell one cell to divide and another to stop dividing?

3
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:21.300
In PC12 cells, EGF drives division, while NGF grows nerve-like fibres.

4
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:27.253
A signal carries duration as well as strength, and ERK helps set its own clock.

5
00:00:28.573 --> 00:00:34.513
Active ERK sends fast negative feedback upstream, phosphorylating SOS and Raf to

6
00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:40.653
weaken them. ERK also induces phosphatases that strip both of its own phosphates.

7
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:44.693
They act slowly because new protein must be made.

8
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:51.067
How long the receptor stays active, plus this feedback, sets how long ERK stays on.

9
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:57.247
In PC12 cells, EGF sparks a brief ERK pulse fading within an hour.

10
00:00:58.607 --> 00:01:00.607
NGF holds ERK high for hours.

11
00:01:01.747 --> 00:01:05.507
Brief ERK goes with division, and sustained ERK with differentiation.

12
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:09.787
How widely other cells use this clock stays open.

13
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.870
Cell context matters just as much as timing.

14
00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:19.970
Researchers placed the same permanently active MEK into two cell types and got

15
00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:26.270
opposite answers. PC12 cells grew neurites with no growth factor at all.

16
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:32.770
Mouse fibroblasts instead piled into disordered foci, a hallmark of transformation

17
00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:33.770
toward cancer.

18
00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:37.613
Human tumours jam this relay higher up.

19
00:01:38.993 --> 00:01:44.593
Ras normally shuts itself off by cutting GTP to GDP, helped by GAP proteins.

20
00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:51.113
A single base change, glycine 12 to valine, blunts that cut, so Ras stays on.

21
00:01:52.273 --> 00:01:56.953
One tier down, many melanomas carry mutant B-Raf V600E.

22
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:02.593
It signals without Ras-GTP, possibly even without a Raf partner.

23
00:02:03.683 --> 00:02:06.203
Now test how pathway state flips a drug.

24
00:02:07.643 --> 00:02:12.583
Vemurafenib plugs the ATP pocket and shuts down B-Raf V600E.

25
00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:18.083
Add that same drug to cells with active Ras and normal B-Raf.

26
00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:21.163
Does ERK fall, stay flat, or rise?

27
00:02:27.117 --> 00:02:29.057
Surprisingly, ERK rises.

28
00:02:30.777 --> 00:02:34.517
Active Ras brings normal Raf molecules into pairs at the membrane.

29
00:02:35.777 --> 00:02:40.917
A drug-bound Raf cannot work itself, yet its contact still pushes the drug-free

30
00:02:40.917 --> 00:02:42.597
partner into an active shape.

31
00:02:44.037 --> 00:02:47.817
Trametinib binds a separate pocket beside MEK's ATP site.

32
00:02:49.157 --> 00:02:53.917
Blocking MEK below Raf helps close that escape, so both drugs are often combined.

33
00:02:55.050 --> 00:03:01.150
Milder germline over-activation causes developmental syndromes called RASopathies,

34
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.370
including Noonan syndrome and neurofibromatosis type 1.

35
00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:13.050
In neurofibromatosis type 1, losing neurofibromin, one of Ras's GAP proteins, leaves

36
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.590
Ras active too long.

37
00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.710
Every cell carries the change, yet tissues differ.

38
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:25.290
Across health and disease, duration, cell context, and pathway state shape what the

39
00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.010
cascade does.
